# SDLC lifecycle gate

This is the spine of how work moves from idea to merge in jobby. The companion
rules own the detail: `30-github-trace` (GitHub is the tracker), `47-docker-first`
(run and deploy only via Compose; k8s later), `48-tailscale-serve` (Funnel/Serve
→ compose-web), `70-engineering-standards`
(clean code / DRY), `80-qa-manual` (manual QA), `90-agent-report-contract` (report format).

## 1. Issue-first — no code before GitHub

A task or requirement is **defined in GitHub before any implementation starts**.

1. Resolve a backlog id `JOBBY-BL-###` from `implementation_backlog.csv` (add a row if none exists).
2. Create the GitHub issue via the **GitHub MCP** (`issue_write`), body includes `<!-- jobby-sync:JOBBY-BL-### -->` and the acceptance criteria.
3. Tag the issue to the active **milestone** (`M#` from `.jobby/ROADMAP.md` → numeric milestone id in `deploy/github/milestones.json`).
4. Only then branch `feature/JOBBY-BL-###-slug` and start coding.

No issue, no milestone, no branch. If the user asks for work that has no issue, create the issue first and say so. GitHub is our backlog and our Jira — see `30-github-trace.mdc` and `.jobby/GITHUB.md`.

## 2. Stage gates — every issue passes through these

Track the stage as a label (`stage:defined`, `stage:designed`, `stage:intake`, `stage:development`, `stage:qa`, `stage:ready`) via `label_write`, advanced left-to-right. Do not skip a gate; a regression found in a later stage goes back to `development`.

| Stage | Done means |
|---|---|
| `defined` | Acceptance criteria written in the issue body; backlog row exists. |
| `designed` | Design refs linked (`deploy/design/*` / ADR) when the change is non-trivial; noted in a comment. |
| `intake` | Dependencies, risks, and the test plan listed in a comment; milestone confirmed. |
| `development` | Branch + code; conventional commits; `node test-all.mjs` green for the touched area. |
| `qa` | Manual + automated QA pass per `80-qa-manual.mdc`; verdict recorded in `qa/runs/`. |
| `ready` | PR open with `Closes #N`; SDLC report `VERDICT` + `SUMMARY` posted; issue closed on merge. |

A merge or stage promotion without a `qa` verdict recorded is **blocked**. "Looks fine" is not a verdict.

## 3. Development format — clean code, DRY

Code follows `70-engineering-standards.mdc` (always applied). The non-negotiables:

- **DRY:** one source of truth. Reuse `web/src/components/job-info-panel.tsx` + `web/src/lib/role-href.ts` for role Job info/status/PDF/Apply; see `.cursor/skills/dry-reuse/SKILL.md`. Do not copy-paste those controls per page.
- **Small diffs:** touch only what the change requires. No unrequested abstractions, no extra files, no new runtime deps (four is a deliberate ceiling).
- **Fail fast with context; never swallow exceptions.** Every network/subprocess call gets an explicit timeout.
- **Tests first:** a bugfix ships its regression test first; behavior that changes scoring or output shape gets a golden run against `evals/`.
- **Conventional commits** (`feat:`, `fix:`, `test:`, …); imperative subject ≤ 72 chars; never hand-edit `CHANGELOG.md` or `VERSION`.
- `.editorconfig` is authoritative: 2-space indent, LF, final newline, no trailing whitespace; tabs in `.go`.

## 4. QA gate before merge

Before each merge or stage promotion, run the manual + automated QA defined in `80-qa-manual.mdc`:

- `npm run qa:coverage` at the start and end of the round; report the percentage and named open criteria, never a raw test count.
- Drive the attended browser with the `playwright` MCP (extension mode) or `browsermcp` (existing Chrome tab) — see `.cursor/skills/qa-manual-automation/SKILL.md` for which to pick and how.
- Record every manual verdict as a ledger line via `scripts/qa-run.mjs`; a manual check that is not recorded did not happen.
- The round prompt is generated (`scripts/qa-prompt.mjs`), never hand-written.

**UI work is blocked from "verified" / `READY_FOR_REVIEW` until attended MCP QA.**
Curl of HTML, RSC greps, unit tests, Docker rebuild, and Cursor's isolated
browser are not that gate. If the Playwright/`browsermcp` tab is missing or
on the wrong URL, the claim is **unverified** — do not invent a substitute.

## 5. Report format at handoff

Bounded delivery, review, QA, release, or handoff work ends with the SDLC report from `.cursor/skills/sdlc-report/SKILL.md`. `TASK_ID` is the GitHub issue `#N` or `JOBBY-BL-###` — never invented. After `VERDICT`, the `SUMMARY` (Arabic first, then English) is required: what this means / what was done / what happens next.

## 6. Traceability — GitHub is the single source of truth

Everything shippable is tracked on `ahmedyusef9/jobby` via the GitHub MCP: issues, milestones, labels, PRs. The CSV is the plan; GitHub is the tracker. Never carry work that lives only in chat or only on a branch — if it isn't in GitHub, it didn't happen. See `30-github-trace.mdc`.

## Diagram

```mermaid
flowchart LR
  BL[implementation_backlog.csv<br/>JOBBY-BL-###] --> ISS[GitHub issue<br/>milestone M#]
  ISS --> ST[stage gates:<br/>defined→designed→intake→development→qa→ready]
  ST --> DEV[clean code + DRY<br/>70-engineering-standards]
  DEV --> QA[qa-manual + qa:coverage<br/>80-qa-manual]
  QA --> PR[PR Closes #N]
  PR --> RPT[SDLC report<br/>VERDICT + SUMMARY]
  RPT --> CLS[issue closed]
```
